    1. delivered_value_per_resource (no upper bound on absolute resource use)
       Q: Is there a cap on any individual resource (e.g., total energy ≤ X kWh, or labor hours cannot exceed the scheduled headcount) so the optimiser cannot inflate absolute consumption while keeping the ratio high — for example, doubling both output and energy use simultaneously?
       A: Apply a per-resource absolute ceiling equal to 110% of the historical shift baseline for each resource category (energy, labor-hours, direct material), preventing runaway consumption while still allowing incremental efficiency gains.
    2. ambient_noise_level (LAeq shift-average vs. short-term peaks)
       Q: The 80 dB limit is defined as a shift-average (LAeq), which permits machines to run at 85–90 dB during production bursts if quieter periods compensate — should a short-term instantaneous ceiling (e.g., 83 dB over any 15-minute window) also be enforced?
       A: Add a secondary short-term limit of 83 dB(A) over any rolling 15-minute window, matching the EU Directive 2003/10/EC upper exposure action value, in addition to the existing 80 dB shift-average constraint.
    3. context.optimization_horizon (shift duration unspecified)
       Q: What is the actual shift length in hours (e.g., 8 h, 10 h, 12 h), including any mandatory breaks, since cumulative fatigue accumulation and the feasibility of the fatigue ≤ 0.4 constraint depend critically on total exposure time and scheduled rest intervals?
       A: Assume a standard 8-hour shift with two 15-minute paid breaks and one 30-minute unpaid meal break; operator-rotation intervals are therefore optimised within six working blocks of approximately 75 minutes each.
    4. assumptions.resource_scope (missing cost weights across resource categories)
       Q: Energy, labor-hours, and direct material are combined into a single denominator — without explicit unit-cost weights (e.g., £/kWh, £/labor-hour, £/kg material), cutting 10% of material looks identical to cutting 10% of energy; should the optimiser use current standard-cost rates, or equal weighting across categories?
       A: Use equal fractional weighting (each resource category contributes one-third of the normalised denominator) until standard-cost rates are provided, preventing unintended over-optimisation of whichever resource happens to carry the largest absolute value.
    5. priority_order vs. _raw_priority_order (intra-HumanWellbeing tie-break missing)
       Q: The top-level priority groups noise and fatigue together under 'HumanWellbeing', but the raw priority order ranks ambient_noise_level above operator_fatigue_index — when both constraints simultaneously approach their limits and relaxing one requires tightening the other (e.g., slowing a noisy machine reduces noise but extends shift duration, increasing fatigue), which should the optimiser protect first?
