  [L1] Ambiguity detection: 5 issue(s) found
    1. priority_order vs. multi_objective_resolution
       Q: Should human-wellbeing constraints (fatigue ≤ 0.4, noise ≤ 80 dB) take absolute lexicographic precedence over resource_efficiency as the primary optimisation objective, or should resource_efficiency be maximised first with wellbeing enforced solely as hard constraints? The contract's priority_order lists only 'HumanWellbeing', yet the multi_objective_resolution assumption says 'maximize resource_efficiency first'—these two directives directly contradict each other and will produce different schedules.
       A: Treat fatigue and noise exclusively as hard constraints (already duplicated in hard_constraints) and maximise resource_efficiency as the single unconstrained KPI objective, resolving the contradiction in favour of the stated optimisation direction.
    2. resource_efficiency.threshold (no minimum target or saturation ceiling)
       Q: Is there a minimum acceptable efficiency gain over the previous shift (e.g., +5 %) or a saturation ceiling above which further gains are not required? Without either bound, the optimiser will push operators and machines to the fatigue and noise limits for every marginal efficiency gain, even when returns are negligible—e.g., a 0.1 % efficiency improvement achieved by running at fatigue index 0.39 continuously for 8 hours.
       A: No ceiling applied; optimiser maximises resource_efficiency freely subject to hard constraints. Previous-shift average is used as the reference baseline per the existing assumption.
    3. resource_efficiency aggregation (value_dimensions and resource_dimensions weights)
       Q: How should the three value dimensions (units_produced, quality_weighted_output, on_time_delivery) and four resource dimensions (energy, raw_materials, labor_hours, machine_time) be weighted in the single efficiency ratio? Different weightings produce fundamentally different optima—e.g., if energy is cheap but labour is scarce, the optimiser might substitute machine-time for labour-hours in a way the manager did not intend.
       A: Equal normalised weighting applied across all value and resource dimensions; if a cost table from the previous accounting period is available it is used instead to convert all dimensions to a common monetary unit before computing the ratio.
    4. operator_fatigue (fatigue_measurement_frequency vs. hard constraint enforcement granularity)
       Q: Does the fatigue hard constraint of ≤ 0.4 apply to the 15-minute rolling average only, or must the instantaneous fatigue index also never exceed 0.4 at any moment? Using only the rolling average allows burst-load sequences where fatigue spikes to ~0.6–0.7 for several minutes while the rolling average stays compliant, which may defeat the intent of the ergonomic guideline.
       A: Constraint enforced on the 15-minute rolling average only, consistent with the existing fatigue_measurement_frequency assumption; no separate instantaneous peak constraint is imposed.
    5. noise_level (temporal measurement basis: instantaneous ceiling vs. time-weighted average)
       Q: Is the 80 dB(A) limit a continuous instantaneous ceiling that must never be exceeded at any operator station at any moment, or a time-weighted average over the shift (as in EU Directive 2003/10/EC Lex,8h), which would permit brief spikes above 80 dB during changeovers or maintenance bursts as long as the shift-average stays compliant?
       A: Treated as a continuous instantaneous ceiling (no exceedance at any moment at any operator station), which is the more conservative interpretation and consistent with the hard_constraints encoding; this may rule out certain changeover procedures that the Directive's time-weighted standard would otherwise permit.

